    "cols2": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "garrett.shue@stryker.com",
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "garrett.shue@stryker.com",
      "jcr:created": "Wed Oct 07 2020 19:36:29 GMT+0000",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Oct 07 2020 19:36:29 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/columncontrol/cols2",
      "col1": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/parsys",
        "text_712876826_copy": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
          "jcr:createdBy": "garrett.shue@stryker.com",
          "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "garrett.shue@stryker.com",
          "jcr:created": "Wed Oct 07 2020 19:30:36 GMT+0000",
          "text": "<p><span class=\"futura-bold\"><span class=\"fontsize-1-5em\">Improving visualization for breast reconstruction</span></span></p>\n<p>Adequate blood flow is crucial to surgical success and the body’s ability to heal. Even a few hours of impaired blood flow can cause damage that can lead to post-operative complications. Advancements in technology, such as our SPY Fluorescence Imaging, can help provide surgeons with real-time visualization of tissue perfusion during breast reconstruction, which may help a surgeon make certain decisions to improve surgical outcomes.<sup>2</sup> SPY-PHI Fluorescence Imaging technology works by using a near-infrared low powered laser light source to stimulate a fluorescent imaging agent that has been injected into&nbsp;a patient's bloodstream. This makes it possible for surgeons to visualize blood flow in real-time.<br>\n<br>\nMeet Barbara, who made the decision to get a bilateral mastectomy and bilateral breast reconstruction when she learned she had an aggressive form of breast cancer. Her surgeon, Dr. Charles Kays, from Wilmington Plastic Surgery, in Wilmington, North Carolina, took advantage of Stryker’s innovative SPY Fluorescence imaging technology during the complicated operation. The reconstructive operation was a success, and Barbara was up and around in just a few days and fully back in action after only four weeks.<span class=\"fontsize-8em\"><span class=\"futura-bold\"><br>\n </span></span></p>\n",
          "jcr:lastModified": "Thu Oct 08 2020 16:58:15 GMT+0000",
          "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
          "textIsRich": "true",
          "cq:responsive": {
            "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
            "default": {
              "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
              "width": "6"
            }
          }
        }
      },
